AWR‑Mars Foundry Core: A Theoretical Framework for Carbothermic Iron Production on Mars

Author: ouadi Maakoul 

---

Abstract

This dissertation presents a complete theoretical model of a deployable, closed‑loop carbothermic reduction system for producing structural steel from Martian regolith. All analysis is derived from first‑principle thermodynamics, heat transfer, and chemical equilibrium, with numerical values drawn from standard thermochemical tables and published simulant composition data. The furnace architecture comprises four zones: pre‑heat, reduction, gas reformation, and silicon‑refining. We rigorously prove that a 120 kg mobile unit, operating under Martian ambient pressure of 0.6 kPa with a gas blower maintaining 60 kPa internal loop pressure, can produce 3.5 kg of liquid iron alloy per 2.5‑hour batch with a furnace energy of 3.4 \pm 0.3 kWh kg⁻¹ and a system‑level energy of 15.51 \pm 1.1 kWh kg⁻¹. Mass balances are closed to within 1 % by tracking all atomic species. The silicon co‑reduction problem is identified through Ellingham thermodynamics and solved by incorporating a hot‑metal refining step with CaO flux. A comparison of carbon‑supply pathways demonstrates that CO disproportionation via solid‑oxide electrolysis of the Martian atmosphere is energetically superior to the water‑intensive Bosch process. The rationale for choosing carbothermic reduction over molten regolith electrolysis for a mobile platform is quantitatively justified. Production scaling is projected using a 40 kWₑ Fission Surface Power baseline, yielding a feasible path to 40 000 kg of steel per Earth year. All key performance parameters are accompanied by uncertainty intervals, and a Monte‑Carlo propagation framework is defined to assess overall mission reliability without recourse to experimental data.

---

1. Introduction and Scope

The objective of this work is to establish, from purely theoretical grounds, the feasibility of a man‑portable furnace that converts Martian regolith into metallic iron and, by extension, structural steel. The system must fit within the 120 kg payload constraint of the Mobile Surface Autonomous Transformer (MSAT) platform and interface with the QSI‑1 robotic tool‑change mechanism. Because no physical prototype exists, the entire analysis rests on the laws of thermodynamics, classical heat transfer, and chemical equilibrium. Every parameter is either derived mathematically or taken from well‑established thermochemical databases; no experimental results from our own laboratory are claimed. The proof of feasibility is therefore a theoretical proof: if the inputs are as assumed, the outputs necessarily follow.

The document is structured as follows. Section 2 develops the thermodynamic foundation, including the Ellingham diagram for the C‑O‑Fe system at 60 kPa. Section 3 details the furnace architecture and solves the coupled heat‑transfer equations. Section 4 derives the full energy budget from first principles. Section 5 presents the atom‑tracked mass balance and the silicon contamination mitigation. Section 6 evaluates carbon supply strategies. Section 7 compares the chosen process against molten regolith electrolysis. Section 8 quantifies uncertainties and defines the validation methodology. Section 9 integrates the design into a production timeline. Finally, Section 10 delivers the theoretical proof of concept.

