2. Thermodynamic Foundations

2.1 Core Reaction Set and Standard Enthalpies

The reduction of ferric oxide by carbon monoxide proceeds as

\mathrm{Fe_2O_3(s) + 3CO(g) \rightarrow 2Fe(l) + 3CO_2(g)} \tag{1}

with standard enthalpy \Delta H_{1}^\circ = -24.8 kJ mol⁻¹ at 298 K. The carbon dioxide is recycled to CO through the Boudouard reaction

\mathrm{CO_2(g) + C(s) \rightleftharpoons 2CO(g)}, \qquad \Delta H_{2}^\circ = +172 \text{ kJ mol}^{-1}. \tag{2}

The net reaction (sum of (1) and three times the reverse of (2)) is the direct carbothermic reduction

\mathrm{Fe_2O_3 + 3C \rightarrow 2Fe + 3CO}, \qquad \Delta H^\circ = +172 \times 3 - 24.8 = +491.2 \text{ kJ mol}^{-1},

which is strongly endothermic and must be driven by electrical heating.

2.2 Equilibrium at Martian Loop Pressure

The furnace operates with an internal gas loop pressurised to 60 kPa by a blower, well above the ambient 0.6 kPa. For reaction (2), the equilibrium constant is

K_p(T) = \frac{p_{\mathrm{CO}}^2}{p_{\mathrm{CO_2}} a_{\mathrm{C}}},

with a_{\mathrm{C}} \approx 1 for solid carbon. Using the standard Gibbs free energy \Delta G^\circ(T) = -RT \ln K_p, and the empirical fit

\Delta G^\circ(T) = 170.7 - 0.174\,T \quad \text{kJ mol}^{-1},

we compute at T=1073 K:

\ln K_p = -\frac{170\,700 - 0.174\times1073\times1000}{8.314\times1073} \approx 2.76 \Rightarrow K_p \approx 15.8.

At total pressure P_{\mathrm{tot}} = 60 kPa, the equilibrium mole fractions satisfy

\frac{y_{\mathrm{CO}}^2}{y_{\mathrm{CO_2}}} = \frac{K_p}{P_{\mathrm{tot}}} = \frac{15.8}{60} \approx 0.263.

With y_{\mathrm{CO}} + y_{\mathrm{CO_2}} = 1, solving yields y_{\mathrm{CO}} \approx 0.92, y_{\mathrm{CO_2}} \approx 0.08. The gas leaving the reduction zone will be rich in CO₂; after passing through the carbon bed it is converted to 92 % CO, with a kinetic effectiveness \eta_{\mathrm{eff}} = 0.85 accounting for pore diffusion, giving a practical conversion of about 85–90 % per pass. This is the basis for the carbon consumption per kilogram of iron.

2.3 Ellingham Analysis and Silicon Contamination

The standard Gibbs free energies of formation for the oxides of iron and silicon are:

2\mathrm{Fe} + \mathrm{O_2} \rightarrow 2\mathrm{FeO}, \quad \Delta G^\circ_{\mathrm{FeO}} = -500 + 0.130\,T \text{ kJ (mol O}_2)^{-1},

\mathrm{Si} + \mathrm{O_2} \rightarrow \mathrm{SiO_2}, \quad \Delta G^\circ_{\mathrm{SiO_2}} = -905 + 0.175\,T \text{ kJ (mol O}_2)^{-1}.

At 1500 °C (1773 K), FeO formation has \Delta G^\circ \approx -500 + 230 = -270 kJ, while SiO₂ formation has \Delta G^\circ \approx -905 + 310 = -595 kJ. In the presence of carbon, the oxygen partial pressure is fixed by the CO/CO₂ ratio. The equilibrium p_{\mathrm{O_2}} is given by

\mathrm{2CO + O_2 \rightleftharpoons 2CO_2}, \quad \Delta G^\circ = -564 + 0.173\,T \text{ kJ},

leading to p_{\mathrm{O_2}} \approx 10^{-10} kPa at 1773 K. Under this reducing potential, both FeO and SiO₂ can be reduced to metal. The Ellingham diagram therefore predicts that at temperatures above ~1400 °C, silicon will co‑reduce into the liquid iron, producing an Fe–Si alloy with 1–8 wt% Si depending on temperature and carbon activity. This is a critical contamination that must be removed to achieve structural steel properties. The thermodynamic necessity of a silicon‑refining step is thus proven.

