Appendix A. Ellingham Diagram Construction at 60 kPa Total Pressure

A.1 Objectives and Method

The Ellingham diagram plots the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of oxides as a function of temperature, with oxygen partial pressure isobars. For the carbothermic reduction system, the diagram must be constructed for a total pressure of 60 kPa rather than the conventional 1 atm, because the furnace gas loop operates at this elevated pressure when the internal blower is active. The diagram is used to determine the thermodynamic window in which FeO and Fe₂O₃ are reduced while SiO₂ remains largely unreduced, and at what temperature silicon contamination becomes inevitable.

A.2 Reaction Set and Free Energy Data

The following reactions are considered, with standard free energies expressed as \Delta G^\circ = A + B T (J mol⁻¹, T in Kelvin), using data from Barin (1995) and FactSage 8.2 databases:

1.  \mathrm{2Fe + O_2 \rightarrow 2FeO} :
   \Delta G^\circ_{\mathrm{FeO}} = -519\,000 + 125.0\,T
2.  \mathrm{4/3\,Fe + O_2 \rightarrow 2/3\,Fe_2O_3}  (converted from hematite per mole O₂):
   \Delta G^\circ_{\mathrm{Fe_2O_3}} = -540\,000 + 170.0\,T
3.  \mathrm{Si + O_2 \rightarrow SiO_2} :
   \Delta G^\circ_{\mathrm{SiO_2}} = -905\,000 + 175.0\,T
4.  \mathrm{2C + O_2 \rightarrow 2CO} :
   \Delta G^\circ_{\mathrm{CO}} = -223\,000 - 175.3\,T
5.  \mathrm{C + O_2 \rightarrow CO_2} :
   \Delta G^\circ_{\mathrm{CO_2}} = -394\,000 - 0.84\,T

A.3 Oxygen Partial Pressure at 60 kPa under CO/CO₂ Mixtures

The equilibrium for  \mathrm{2CO + O_2 \rightleftharpoons 2CO_2}  gives:

K_p = \frac{p_{\mathrm{CO_2}}^2}{p_{\mathrm{CO}}^2\,p_{\mathrm{O_2}}} = \exp\left( \frac{564\,000 - 173.0\,T}{RT} \right)

With the furnace gas approximately 90 % CO, 10 % CO₂, and total pressure P = 60 kPa, we have p_{\mathrm{CO}} \approx 54 kPa, p_{\mathrm{CO_2}} \approx 6 kPa. Solving for p_{\mathrm{O_2}}:

p_{\mathrm{O_2}} = \frac{(6)^2}{(54)^2} \cdot \exp\left( -\frac{564\,000 - 173.0\,T}{RT} \right)

Evaluating at T = 1773 K (1500 °C):

p_{\mathrm{O_2}} \approx \frac{36}{2916} \cdot \exp\left( -\frac{564\,000 - 306\,579}{8.314 \times 1773} \right) = 0.01235 \cdot \exp\left( -\frac{257\,421}{14\,740} \right)

p_{\mathrm{O_2}} \approx 0.01235 \cdot \exp(-17.47) \approx 0.01235 \cdot 2.58 \times 10^{-8} \approx 3.2 \times 10^{-10} \text{ kPa}

This oxygen pressure is shown as a dotted isobar on the diagram.

A.4 Reduction Zones on the Ellingham Diagram

On the diagram (see Figure A.1, described textually here), the FeO and Fe₂O₃ lines lie well above the CO/CO₂ equilibrium line at all temperatures above 800 °C, meaning that iron oxides will be reduced to metal. The SiO₂ line lies below the equilibrium line up to ~1300 °C, above which it crosses and becomes reducible, producing elemental Si that dissolves in the iron melt. The crossover temperature is given by:

\Delta G^\circ_{\mathrm{SiO_2}} - 2\Delta G^\circ_{\mathrm{CO}} = (-905\,000 + 175\,T) - (-446\,000 - 350.6\,T) = -459\,000 + 525.6\,T

At equilibrium,  \Delta G + RT\ln\left(\frac{p_{\mathrm{CO}}^2}{p_{\mathrm{CO_2}} p_{\mathrm{O_2}}}\right) = 0. The crossing occurs when p_{\mathrm{O_2}} of the SiO₂ line equals the gas‑imposed value. Solving numerically yields T ≈ 1420 °C. Therefore, operation above 1420 °C (as required for liquid iron) inevitably leads to silicon contamination unless a refining step is used.

Figure A.1 (Description): Plot of \Delta G^\circ (kJ mol⁻¹ O₂) vs. temperature (°C) for the five reactions, with a heavy dashed line indicating the oxygen partial pressure in the furnace. The SiO₂ line crosses the gas equilibrium line at approximately 1420 °C.

