Appendix D. QSI‑1 Tool Interface Detailed Design

D.1 Purpose and System Context

The Quick‑Swap Interface (QSI‑1) provides a single‑action robotic coupling between the AWR‑Mars rover arm and the foundry tool heads (RH‑2 Harvester, IC‑1 Crucible, LA‑3 Laser). The interface simultaneously mates electrical power (50 A at 400 V), optical data (single‑mode fibre, 10 Gbps), and mechanical load (shear strength 5 000 N), across the Martian thermal range (−125 °C to +150 °C).

D.2 Dual‑Taper Wedge Lock Geometry

The design uses a primary taper of 10° and a secondary taper of 5°. The actuation train converts a 625 N motor force via a harmonic drive (ratio 400:1) and ball screw, generating a normal contact force of:

F_n = \frac{625}{2\sin(10^\circ)} \approx 1800 \text{ N}

over a total contact area of 36 mm², yielding a contact pressure of 50 MPa. The gold‑plated beryllium‑copper contacts have a measured specific contact resistance of <0.1 mΩ at this pressure, ensuring a total electrical loop resistance below 0.2 mΩ.

D.3 Optical Alignment Subsystem

A piezoelectric stage with 20 μm stroke corrects the residual lateral alignment error after the wedge is fully seated. The alignment target is ±100 μm at the fibre ferrule. Thermal expansion from the mounting structure is compensated by using Invar 36 carriers, with a CTE of 1.2×10⁻⁶ K⁻¹, limiting the displacement due to a 275 K swing to less than 20 μm. The optical power budget includes a 0.5 dB coupling loss at 1310 nm.

D.4 Calculated Interface Performance Parameters

· Electrical: 50 A DC, voltage drop <10 mV, power loss <0.5 W.
· Optical: Insertion loss <1.0 dB (including connector and alignment).
· Mechanical: Shear capacity 12 kN (safety factor 2.4 on 5 000 N operational load).
· Thermal: Operational from −125 °C to +150 °C, survival to +200 °C.
· Duty cycle: >10 000 mate/demate cycles without degradation (based on gold‑on‑gold contact wear data).

D.5 Integration with Control System

The QSI‑1 approach sequence uses Hall‑effect sensors for coarse alignment (±5 mm), a camera with LED target for fine pre‑alignment (±0.5 mm), and the dual‑taper wedges for final lock. Feedback from contact resistance measurements (<0.2 mΩ threshold) confirms successful electrical connection. The entire sequence is managed by the AIADN control loop and can be completed in under 30 seconds.

