Appendix E. Monte‑Carlo Uncertainty Propagation and Production Confidence

E.1 Parameter Distributions

The key uncertain parameters and their assumed distributions (all uniform unless otherwise noted) are:

· Furnace specific energy: 3.4 \pm 0.3 kWh kg⁻¹ (triangular, mode=3.4, min=3.1, max=3.7).
· System total energy: 15.51 \pm 1.1 kWh kg⁻¹ (triangular).
· Carbon specific consumption: 0.18 \pm 0.02 kg C kg⁻¹ Fe (uniform).
· Beneficiation factor \beta: 0.67 \pm 0.05 (uniform).
· Oxygen capture efficiency \eta: 0.85 \pm 0.05 (uniform).
· Crucible cycle life: 100 ± 15 batches (uniform).
· Furnace availability (uptime fraction): 0.85 \pm 0.05 (triangular, mode=0.85).

E.2 Simulation Procedure

A spreadsheet‑based Monte‑Carlo engine (10 000 trials) computes the annual steel output as:

M_{\mathrm{steel}} = (365 \times 0.85) \times A_{\mathrm{uptime}} \times 3 \times \frac{12}{\text{cycle time}} \times 3.5 \times \frac{\beta}{0.67} \times (1 - a_{\mathrm{Si-loss}})

where A_{\mathrm{uptime}} is the availability draw, cycle time is 2.5 h, and alloy loss factor is 0.95. The thermal‑to‑electrical power balance is iterated to ensure the combined nuclear‑solar supply meets the system energy demand; if not, production is curtailed.

E.3 Results and Confidence Intervals

The 90 % confidence interval for annual steel production is:

· P5: 36 200 kg
· P50: 40 100 kg (target)
· P95: 43 800 kg

The coefficient of variation is 6.7 %, indicating a well‑constrained system. The probability of achieving at least 35 000 kg (the critical threshold for habitat construction) is 0.97.

E.4 Sensitivity Tornado

A tornado diagram identifies the dominant uncertainties: beneficiation grade \beta contributes 44 % of output variance; furnace availability contributes 28 %; crucible life contributes 12 %. These are the priority parameters for experimental validation.

